thena spine> :parse LC ( λ x : ι . x )
LC.abs(x, Ty.base, LC.var(x))
thena spine> :parse LC (λf:(ι->ι).(λy:ι.f y))
LC.abs(f, Ty.arrow(Ty.base, Ty.base), LC.abs(y, Ty.base, LC.app(LC.var(f), LC.var(y))))
thena spine> :parse LC ( λ █ : ι . █ )
LC.abs(█, Ty.base, █)
thena spine> :parse LC f a b
in LC`f a b`: this term parses two ways, as LC.app(LC.var(f), LC.app(LC.var(a), LC.var(b))) and as LC.app(LC.app(LC.var(f), LC.var(a)), LC.var(b))
thena spine> :parse LC { a a }
LC.repeat(LC.var(a), LC.var(a))
thena spine> :parse LC { a b }
in LC`{ a b }`: LC.repeat's M is written more than once and must read the same each time, but here it is LC.var(a) and LC.var(b)
thena spine> :parse LC ( λ x ι )
in LC`( λ x ι )`: unexpected 'ι' at character 7, expecting :
thena spine> :parse LC ( λ x : ι .
in LC`( λ x : ι .`: the term ends too soon, expecting x, ( or {
thena spine> :parse Ty ( ι -> ( ι -> ι ) )
Ty.arrow(Ty.base, Ty.arrow(Ty.base, Ty.base))
thena spine> :parse Nope x
no language called Nope has been declared
thena spine> :parse LC
each line is an LC term: Tab shows what fits at the cursor, █ is a part not written yet, :done leaves
parse LC> ( λ x : ι . x )
LC.abs(x, Ty.base, LC.var(x))
parse LC> f a b
in LC`f a b`: this term parses two ways, as LC.app(LC.var(f), LC.app(LC.var(a), LC.var(b))) and as LC.app(LC.app(LC.var(f), LC.var(a)), LC.var(b))
parse LC> :parse LC
in LC`:parse LC`: unexpected ':' at character 1, expecting x, ( or {
parse LC> :done
done parsing LC
thena spine> :parse LC ( λ y : ι . y )
LC.abs(y, Ty.base, LC.var(y))
